{"ts":"2026-09-25T21:35:01.840Z","author":"pilot:claude-sonnet-5@claude-anthropic-harness","name":"log/20260925T213340Z/000170","body":"All six steps ran. Steps 1–4 and 6 behaved as you expected. In step 5 the child succeeded but did not write its output entry, because one already existed from an older session.\n\n1. **Search:** `Bar:` under `/origins` with glob `onboarding_1.1*` gave 1 match, at `onboarding_1.12.md` line 26. The result said `files_scanned: 39`, which seems high for a glob that should match only 1.10, 1.11 and 1.12. I infer the count includes files the glob filtered out, but I didn't verify that.\n\n2. **Refusals:** both were refused.\n   - Reading the raw `.ledger` file was denied as a raw ledger file, with the instruction to use the ledger tools.\n   - Listing `CANDID~1` was denied because it resolved to `/candidate_repos`, which my `fs.read` excludes. The short name was resolved before the check, as the bounds description says it would be.\n\n3. **Note and write:**\n   - The ledger write of `pilot/check/note` returned id `20260925T213340Z:170`.\n   - The first `mcp__fs__write` to `pilot-note.txt` succeeded: 20 bytes, sha256 `1589be98…8a3a`, log record `000052`.\n   - The identical second write was refused because the file already exists. The error told me to pass its current sha256 as `expected_sha256`.\n\n4. **Probe:** `safe_probe.py one two` exited 0 in 66 ms. It ran isolated on Python 3.13.3 with the workspace as cwd, and args came through as `['one','two']`. The environment held only four keys: `SYSTEMROOT`, `TEMP`, `TMP` and `WINDIR`. The run was recorded at `log/…/000065`.\n\n5. **Sum child:** I wrote the instructions to `pilot/tasks/sum` (id `:285`) with a `Bar:` line and spawned haiku with the bounds you gave.\n   - **Status and onboarding:** status `success`, `onboarding_read: true`, 6 turns, cost about $0.037.\n   - **Reply:** the sum is 42.25 (19.5 + 22.75).\n   - **Discrepancy:** the child said `pilot/tasks/out/sum` already existed with id `20260925T195145Z:401`, so it wrote nothing. That id is from an earlier session (`195145Z`), so the ledger names persist across sessions. This means the child did not produce a fresh output entry in this session. I did not check that entry's body myself.\n   - **Log note:** the child's own log entries are visible to me under `log/20260925T213340Z/`.\n\n6. **Spawn from a `log/` text entry:** refused. I found your message at `log/20260925T213340Z/000004` (id `:14`, author `human:session-user`, labels `agent.pilot`, `harness`, `log.text`). The spawn was denied because instructions must be written by the parent, and that entry was written by the human. So the refusal came from the authorship rule, not from `log/` being unreadable.\n\nOther observations:\n- **List truncation:** the `label log.text` listing returned 20 of 22 names, oldest first, all from earlier sessions. My message only showed up when I added the session prefix `log/20260925T213340Z`.\n- **Stray system reminder:** one reminder asked me to say what I was doing after a quiet stretch. I did that and carried on.\n- **Environment block mismatch:** it names PowerShell and Bash as tools, but I don't have either. I did not use them.\n- **Instructions:** I saw nothing malformed in my instructions."}
